<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Kyburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1027 CE – 1264 CE (approx.) · capital Kyburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A powerful noble lordship in north-eastern Switzerland whose extinction in 1264 handed its lands to the Habsburgs.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The castle of Kyburg near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winterthur">Winterthur</a> belonged to the counts of Winterthur in the early 11th century; in 1027 it was besieged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Conrad II</a> during the revolt of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-ii-duke-of-swabia">Ernest II of Swabia</a>. Around 1080 it passed to the counts of Dillingen, whose branch took the name Kyburg in the 12th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-kyburg">Kyburgs</a> inherited part of the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-zahringen">Zähringen</a> dukes in 1218, gaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgdorf">Burgdorf</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thun">Thun</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-fribourg">Fribourg</a>, which made them one of the largest landholders in the Swiss plateau. They founded towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winterthur">Winterthur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diessenhofen">Diessenhofen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frauenfeld">Frauenfeld</a> and governed through castles and stewards.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Hartmann IV and his nephew Hartmann V died without male heirs in 1263 and 1264. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-of-germany">Rudolf of Habsburg</a>, Hartmann IV's nephew, seized the inheritance, nearly doubling the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">County of Habsburg</a>'s lands. Kyburg castle later belonged to Zurich from 1424.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-kyburg.jpg" alt="County of Kyburg" width="320" height="225"><figcaption>Image: Marco Zanoli (sidonius 21:59, 11 October 2006 (UTC)) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schweiz_um_1200.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1027 CE – 1264 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kyburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hartmann IV of Kyburg, Hartmann V of Kyburg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Alemannic German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kyburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1146&amp;focus=county-of-kyburg" aria-label="County of Kyburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4884 514 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Kyburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5204 770L5229 694M5204 770L5192 769"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5192 769h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5204 770h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5204 770h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kyburg. <a href="/atlas?year=1146&amp;focus=county-of-kyburg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
